    "turn_idx": 0,
    "turn_text": "Welcome to plain talk. I'm your host, Rob Port. Happy to be with you. Joining me later in the program is Senator Kevin Kramer, who is on for his first, his very first appearance on the podcast. Now, the reason why Kramer is kind of important to the show back when I was doing terrestrial radio on W.D.A.Y. Congressman, then Congressman Kramer was on the show every week and he took phone calls. And I wanted to replicate that on the podcast. Now, obviously, we can't do live phone calls because we prerecord the podcast and then distribute it. And that's part of the convenience for you, the listeners is you get to listen to this show whenever the heck you want. But that also means it's hard to do live calling. So what we've been doing is if you follow me on Facebook, if you follow me on Twitter and you can find me there, just search for Rob Port. You'll find me. become my friend. You'll see I'll start putting up posts and I'll solicit questions from you. So if there's something that you want to run by Senator Kramer, you can do so. You can post it on one of those threads. I'll monitor them and I'll ask the questions. You can also email them to me, robitsayanythingblog.com. And the great thing about Having Kramer on the podcast is what I'm asking him the questions. We don't have a commercial break bearing down on us that we have to get to. Uh, we could give him time to fully answer your question. So to me, it's a, it's a service really to you, the listeners. He's your Senator. He's my Senator too. And this is a way for you to get access to him and ask questions and have him answer them candidly for you. And I honestly frankly, I think it's a lot of fun. And as a bonus, Congressman Armstrong, who replaced Senator Kramer in the United States House of Representatives, is gonna be doing the same thing. He'll be on later this week as well. And we're gonna make this a weekly thing. We're gonna do it as often as we can and just give you guys access to these fellows and let them answer your questions. So that's coming up later in the program. But before we get to that, I got a little bit of a rant on a debate going on in Bismarck. There's a John Hageman article that was out earlier this week. Headline was, despite pleas for road funding, North Dakota Senate rejects gas tax increase. The legislation was set up bill 2288. It failed on a 26 to 18 vote. It was pushed by Republican Senator Larry Lewis. He's from Fairmount, North Dakota. It would have increased North Dakota's taxes on motor vehicle and special fuels to 27 cents per gallon. Now, what's interesting is this hasn't been changed since 2005. So we're going on like 14 years. of the same tax that we've had the entire time. By the way, the federal tax of 18.4 cents per gallon hasn't been increased since 1993. Now, as introduced, the bill would have increased fuel taxes to 30 cents per gallon, but it was amended down. It would have the amended version, which ultimately failed, but it would have generated $59.2 million in revenue during the two-year budget cycle. So it was about a $60 million tax hike. Now it was voted down. I will tell you that I am, and by the way, there's other bills like this before the legislature. There was a, and by the way, there's another bill to raise the gas tax, 230 cents per gallon. It was introduced by a Democratic lawmaker that's still alive in the house. There's also a bill that the Senate's going to be considering to consider raising driver's license fees. And there is, you know, I guess what I'm trying to say is I'm a conservative person. You're not very often going to hear me advocating for the government taking more of our money. That being said, I think we do reach a point in some of these debates, where if we're not willing to raise any of these fees, this is what puts upward pressure on taxes like the income tax, taxes like the sales tax. Now I will tell you, I don't necessarily mind the gas tax. I understand why the politics around it are so hard. A lot of people don't like it because fuel prices are one of the most visible economic prices in our economy. They're literally posted alongside the roads. You drive up and down the roads, you see gas stations advertising their fuel prices. So when they go up, they go down, it's very, very visible. Now, and by the way, we all have to use fuel. I mean, it's like the cost of food, too. It's like the cost of bread. It's one of those things. It's not really a fungible commodity. We all have to have fuel. Some amount, we got to get to work. Those prices are built in. They affect the economy. So if fuel prices go up, it's more expensive for all of us. I get that. I understand it. But I like the fuel tax in that it is essentially a user tax, right? Now, we're running into some problems. And as a matter of fact, that's another bill before the legislature. I believe it passed in the house, if I'm remembering correctly. Anyway, or maybe it was a Senate. Anyway, it was basically a bill which would have imposed a fee on electric vehicles. And the fee is essentially in lieu of the fact that these electric vehicles are not paying the fuel tax. Because what the fuel tax goes towards is maintaining our roads. which is why I like the fuel tax, because despite the difficulties with some emerging manners of propelling our motor vehicles that are on the roads, it amounts to a user fee, right? So if you drive a lot on the roads, you buy more fuel, you pay more tax. Generally, I like that. I like user fees. I like when we can take an expensive government and tie it as closely as possible to the people using that government service. So in the instance of the roads, the people who use the roads the most are paying the most tax. That's great. That's how it should be. but because the politics around this, and it's almost, I guess what I could compare it to is sort of a nimby attitude, right? You know that acronym, N-I-M-B-Y, not in my backyard, mostly you see it crop up in people not wanting, you know, Somebody who they don't want some no development in their area, right? They don't want a new apartment building in their area. They don't want a new, you know, landfill near their property or whatever. So it's the not in my backyard attitude. And I think it's almost comparable to that. This attitude about taxes, right? Where people don't want to pay a higher fuel tax. So we never raise the fuel tax again in North Dakota. The fuel tax hasn't been raised since 2005 at the federal level. It hasn't been raised since 1993. That's more than two decades. That's a little scary. Why aren't we raising these taxes? And it's because of politics, which leads me back to another theme that I return too often in my podcasts and in my writing, which is that I think democracy and free markets are very, very good at delivering the American public what they want, even if it's something that they don't admit that they actually want. So for instance, what happens is, is because there's so much political resistance to raising, say, the fuel tax or raising, you know, other sorts of taxes or whatever, there's so much resistance to it. But yet simultaneously, we all want lots of government programs. We all want like really nice roads and rest areas and no potholes and well maintained and we want the plowed and all that good stuff. These two things are contradictory. You can't have well-maintained roads and then really low taxes. You can't have two of these things. If you want well-maintained roads, you're going to have to pay for them. That goes across government. If you want lots of government services, you're going to have to pay for them. And what happens is through politics and through this sort of, because we don't really have a lot of very principal voters in this country, what we'll see is people voting for expansive programs. They want luxurious roads, they want expansive government programs, they want lots of government services, and they want low taxes. Now, they don't necessarily mind if somebody else's taxes gets hiked, which is why it's so popular. You'll hear politicians talking all the time about taxing the rich or taxing the corporations or, you know, doing this, that or the other thing, cutting foreign aid. of these are really workable solutions to our budget and national debt and deficit problems, but they sound good. They're good politics, right? In reality, what should be happening is we all should have some skin in the game when it comes to paying for our government. I firmly believe that. I think everybody should pay taxes. I don't necessarily like progressive tax codes. I like tax codes that keeps everybody sub-skin in the game when it comes to paying for the government. Now, I don't necessarily mind that people who make less pay less, that people who make more pay more. I think that's probably okay, but generally everybody should pay something. And when government gets bigger, everybody should pay a little bit more because we should understand that the government belongs to all of us, right? What is that famous quote for President Obama? Government is a name for the things that we do together. I'm not sure I believe in that statement in the way that he meant to believe in it, but in terms of the fact that the government we're all responsible for paying for it, I believe in it that way. And I believe that if we're going to vote to make the government bigger, If we're gonna vote for the government to do more things, then it should cost us more, and it should cost everybody more, and we should all agree, okay, well that's what we're gonna pay in exchange for getting more from the government. But then getting back to the fuel taxes, that's why I simultaneously support the idea of user fees. So tie the cost of the government program in this instance, roads as closely as possible to the people getting the most benefit from it. That way, you know, if we need to spend more on roads to maintain them, we need to build more roads. We need to build wider roads. We need to do spend more on maintaining them or clear whatever it is. The people who are going to pay the most are the people who use it the most. That's fair. That's fairness. And so it doesn't necessarily always have to be, well, we gotta spend more on roads, so we're gonna raise taxes on everybody. It's, no, we're gonna spend more on roads, and we're gonna raise the tax that the people who use the roads the most pay. This makes sense to me. This is logical to me. This seems like a very sound way to govern, and yet we don't like this because of populism, because of populist politics, because of the fact that what Americans really want, even though we say we don't like deficits and debt, what we really like is low taxes or somebody else paying the taxes, while we get expansive government and lots of government services. And the thing is, is that generally the government delivers it, not so much here at the state level, although resistance to raising North Dakota's fuel taxes, despite there being a pressing need for more spending on our roads, is an example of that. But at the federal level, we have the perfect example, because what do we get year after year after year? We get the government delivering exactly what the America taxpayer wants. expansive government, low taxes. And then what happens, we run a budget deficit in the hundreds of billions of dollars every year, sometimes even in the trillions of dollars. That's democracy, giving us exactly what we want. That's populism, giving us exactly what we want because we spend so much time talking about the wisdom of the masses. We spend so much time talking about the will of the people. Well, what if the will of the people is irresponsible? What if the will of the people is the equivalent to you financing your household budget on credit cards? I mean, what if that's the will of the people? Sometimes the will of the people is not a good idea. I understand the counter-argument to that. What should we just turn everything over to benevolent dictators? Should we turn everything over to sub-tech to credit elite to run things for us? Well, no, I don't necessarily agree with that either, but at some point, I guess what I'm saying is we the public have to be willing to say, you know what? If we want better roads, we'd better pay for them. We want better schools. We better be willing to pay for them. And to the extent that we want to manage what, you know, sort of financial impacts are on us so that we don't, you know, spend our ways and ourselves into poverty or spend ourselves into economic stagnation because it's so expensive to start a business. It's so expensive to live a life. Well, then, you know, we do things like user fees or we do things like, you know, making sure that the government is narrowly focused on the things that we really want them to do and not off doing all these other extracurricular things that really the government has no business doing in the first place. But we've got to get this nimby attitude out of politics. And I don't know that nimby's the right word for it, but we've got to get this attitude out of politics where it's, you know, I don't want to raise any taxes, but simultaneously I want the government to do lots and lots of things. If you want the government to do lots and lots of things, then you got to be willing to pay for it. And you know what? You should be willing to pay for it yourself. Not not. I want the government to give me lots of things and then tax those people over there for it. No, that doesn't work. That's part of the responsibility of being a citizen, I think, is being willing to pay for the government. Now, personally, because I'm a conservative, I like the government to do very little and to tax me very little. And I'm willing to accept that the government is going to provide me with fewer services. And in turn, I will be willing to deal with that and enjoy my lower tax burdens. Now, I know not everybody's going to agree with me, but that should be the standpoint. I'm willing to accept that trade-off, the government doing less in exchange for me getting taxed less. Simultaneously, if you want the government to do more, you should be willing to allow the government to tax you more. And if we all were principled in that way, I feel like our country would work a lot better. But instead, we all pretend like we are principled in that way when we're not really, and we could tell we're not really because the politicians",
