  {
    "episode_id": 47218,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47218.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 49,
    "turn_text": "Well, every time I agree with that principle and that statement's pretty well put, I'm reminded that he's Donald Trump because he doesn't do those things, that there is that other part of him that is attractive to people who are tired of politicians and that there's actually a trust in his authenticity when he says what other people wish they could say. But with regard to the focus on himself, that part has always been a little bit bothersome to me because just take as an example. If I might just sort of dissect it a little bit knowing how dangerous this is. His references to his handling of North Korea, I don't think anybody can deny that Donald Trump has brought Kim Jong Un to the negotiating table. He has overseen 15 months of relative peace with North Korea that's been uncommon. He's got him coming back for doing another summit. All of that's good news, the lack of testing of missiles, the return of hostages and remains. All of that's good news that stands on its own. But then when he says, if I hadn't been elected president, we'd be at war with North Korea. I don't know how you'd know that, for sure. And it's not like we were on the brink of war with North Korea. before he became president, it's just that we were sliding into a very bad place in a very unsafe peninsula. And I think his accomplishments are so good that sort of taking a victory lap like that's just unnecessary and then many times diminishes the actual accomplishment itself.",
