    "turn_idx": 52,
    "turn_text": "Well, I was very impressed. And I wasn't surprised that I would be impressed. But when you're talking to a guy who had been attorney general, several presidents ago really now. And you think, well, why is he even doing this? I mean, he must have a servant's heart and all that and all the things I've ever heard about him were true. But I also really did want to prod him on this issue of cooperative federalism and the Consolidated Litigation Authority of the Department of Justice. Two things that have frustrated me, two things that sort of go hand in hand. And I cited very specific cases. And so that people know I sent him the questions prior to him coming in, so that he could be prepared for that. Not that I need them to know every detail of the North Dakota cases, but more importantly, I wanted them to know his philosophy, not just on governing, but on management of the Department of Justice, because I think It's been pretty clear that the Department of Justice in many respects has become a bit of its own client and has forgotten that its responsibility is to the people that they work for and represent. And so the cases I cited, the one of course being the Environmental Integrity Project versus EPA case that was filed in California, but it was challenging North Dakota's North Dakota's enforcement, what's called rick rot laws. It's a solid waste. It's a solid waste case. And of course, when you file something like that in the Ninth Circuit with a liberal judge and you're a liberal organization, you're going to get a pretty good ruling. And in their case, it was actually a sewage settle ruling, which are always the worst because North Dakota then filed to intervene in the case and was denied by the judge in California. Well, that was all under the Obama administration. Fast forward. the North Dakota appeals the case to the DC Circuit Court of Appeals, or it gets appealed to the DC Circuit Court of Appeals. So North Dakota goes to DC Circuit to try and get the right to intervene. And now it's the Trump administration. And in the meantime, Scott Pruitt, who's the EPA administrator, bans all sue and settle cases. We're not going to go to friendly courts and settle apart from the people that are affected by the settlement. And so the Department of Justice might be should have been advocating that position. Instead, they advocated for the Obama position. And the reason that bothers me is because, obviously, it completely ignores North Coast right to intervene in the case that directly affects their authority with its primacy to enforce these laws. That's number one. But number two, the number two case is a case dealing with Billings County federal lands out in Billings County and Billings County's right to easements for roads and whatnot, which is, in my view, pretty clear law and tradition. And I lost it there that back in 2000, that was actually, you know, the sort of the clock ran out on for North Dakota, while the USDA attorneys delayed and delayed and delayed. Well, we continue to try and fix that problem. But every time you go to the USDA, they say, well, DOJ attorneys, they won't last do it. You go to DOJ and they say, well, they could do it. But it's up to the USDA attorneys. And all of this, because of a law back in the 60s, it consolidated. For reasonable reasons, consolidated DOJ consolidated federal government litigation under the Department of Justice. But unlike North Dakota, which I think has a good model. where North Dakota, every agency has an attorney who's an assistant attorney",
