    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47218.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 58,
    "turn_text": "Well, first of all, When you take your salary and give it to charity and then make an announcement about the charity you gave it to or you announced it, I'm so noble that this month I'm giving my salary to charity. I don't find that, and I don't mean to be critical of my friends who do it, but I don't find it very noble, frankly. In fact, I'm surprised that people think it is noble. What do they do with their salary to the other 11 months? How much of that do they? contributed. Do they tide 10% to their church before paying taxes and all of those things? I just don't want to get into that rut. I take a biblical view, frankly, of charity. And I'm not even real big on naming buildings after people have argued soliciting money for people to have their name on the building. I've done plenty of it as a fundraiser for for the University of Mary and other charities that I've worked for. I understand that there's an appeal of that to some people, and that that actually becomes a tool. It's never been mine at a personal level. So that's part of my problem with it. It just makes it easy. Money's fungible. So I could easily say that I gave my salary last month to charity, and no one would know the difference. I give plenty of money over the course of the year that more than cover that amount of money, but that's not the point. The other thing about it is that I really, it really concerns me about this whole notion. First of all, the Congress did work. We worked. I mean, I went to many hearings and briefings and",
