    "turn_idx": 66,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. So thanks for the question, Sarah. And by the way, thanks for the illustration, because it's like many of them that I've heard about. It's like many of what I consider the forgotten people in the whole Obamacare, you know, enthusiasm, because if you would through your work and been getting healthcare, which by the way, well over half of the people in North Dakota, that's how they get their healthcare. And for those who are tempted to be tempted by the lure of Medicare for all or the Camilla Harris healthcare plans, they would eliminate all of that. All of that would go away. Not only would it cost you more, you wouldn't even be able to get it. Your employer wouldn't be able to give it to you. I mean, some of them don't want any private insurance In which case, you'd have no marketplace. You'd have no competition. You'd have no natural market incentives. What you'd have is inferior health care. Free to accept to all the taxpayers. So what's coming next? Well, we've done a few things. Why don't we do the task as a jobs act, eliminate the individual mandate in the most recent lawsuit. As a result of that, the lawsuit under the constitutionality of Obamacare, the whole thing. It has been ruled by a judge in Texas to be unconstitutional, although he did not stay it. So no doubt it will be appealed. This moment opens the window that lasts for probably at least two years, maybe more, depending on how quickly we get to the Supreme Court. But it presents an incentive for people on all sides to come up with a solution that through replacing some of those incentives that Sarah's probably referring to provided all kinds of different options for people. That's at the end of the day, that's about mine. For me personally, what I want to see us get back to is back to something more like that that gives more than authority to states and provides the tools either to the tax code or or subsidies or whatever the case might be through through the existing existing mechanisms to incent states to to innovate, you know, where the state innovation grants, something that our governor was looking forward to.",
